// uidesc.cla: the FOUNDATIONAL file for the ported UI runtime (native-5e
// Task 7 Step 2) -- everything else this port adds (ui.cla/uiwidgets.cla's
// real bodies, Step 3+; uiscript.cla, Step 3) depends on the three things
// this file provides: read-only accessor functions over the UI descriptor
// blob (clarusc/uiblob.cla builds it; this is the runtime-side reader for
// the SAME format, see that file's header comment for the authoritative
// section-layout spec -- condensed below), the `RtUiWinst`/`RtUiCanvasBuf`
// overlay records a ported window instance's memory is shaped as, and
// `rtUiAllocLocked` (the one shared allocation primitive both use). Spliced
// FIRST in main.cla's --uiport manifest block, before ui.cla/uiwidgets.cla
// -- same "foundational types first" convention str->text->list->map
// already established (see Task 7 report log entry #1).
//
// ================================================================
// The UI descriptor blob (reader side)
// ================================================================
//
// Format (normative source: clarusc/uiblob.cla's own header comment; this
// is the runtime-side mirror of that spec, not a second definition of it).
// EVERY field in every section is a big-endian int32 at a 4-byte-aligned,
// BLOB-ABSOLUTE byte offset (never section-relative) -- bools/counts/flags
// included, no packed sub-int32 field anywhere. -1 marks an absent/
// optional reference (no title, no form, an empty array, ...). Strings are
// Str255 (length byte + raw bytes, no padding) in a trailing string-pool
// section, referenced by their own blob-absolute byte offset.
//
// Section order + field layout. Form/Bind/Table/Col/Layout/EnumArrays
// (below the App section) were OUT OF SCOPE through Task 8 (no
// field/textview/popup/table binding existed yet) -- Task 9 adds their
// accessors, needed by uitable.cla's table/popup code:
//   Header (11 int32 @ 0,4,..,40): magic, version, nWins, winsOff, nMenus,
//     menusOff, nMenuHandlers, mhOff, nEvery, everyOff, appOff.
//   Window (13 int32, 52 bytes, base = winsOff + winIdx*52): nameOff,
//     titleOff, w, h, resizable, minW, minH, nWidgets, widgetsOff,
//     stateSize, handlerMask, formOff (-1 if the window has no `form for T`),
//     menuMask (bit i = declared menu i is claimed by this window type via
//     `menus:`; 0 for a window with no such clause).
//   Widget (13 int32, 52 bytes, base = widgetsOff + widgetIdx*52): kind,
//     nameOff, captionOff, atKind, x, yIsBottom, y, widthIsFill, width,
//     fillBoth, flags, eventMask, tableOff (-1 unless kind == table).
//   Menu (5 int32, 20 bytes, base = menusOff + menuIdx*20): nameOff,
//     titleOff, nItems, itemsOff, isStandardEdit.
//   Item (4 int32, 16 bytes, base = a menu's own itemsOff + itemIdx*16):
//     nameOff, labelOff, key, separator.
//   MenuHandler (6 int32, 24 bytes, base = mhOff + k*24): menuIdx, itemIdx,
//     handlerIdx, scopeWinIdx, menuNameOff, itemNameOff.
//   Every (1 int32, 4 bytes, base = everyOff + k*4): ticks.
//   App (4 int32, 16 bytes, at appOff if appOff != -1): nameOff,
//     versionOff, authorOff, aboutOff.
//   Form (3 int32, 12 bytes, at a window's own formOff): layoutOff, nBinds,
//     bindsOff (-1 if nBinds == 0).
//   Bind (2 int32, 8 bytes, base = a form's own bindsOff + k*8):
//     widgetIndex, fieldIndex.
//   Table (4 int32, 16 bytes, at a widget's own tableOff): rowsIdx (the
//     UiTableRows extern's own argument -- clarusc/uiblob.cla's own
//     "rowsIdx" header note), layoutOff, nCols, colsOff (-1 if nCols == 0).
//   Col (4 int32, 16 bytes, base = a table's own colsOff + k*16):
//     headerOff, widthPx, widthFill, fieldIndex.
//   Layout (2 + nFields*6 int32, at a form's or table's own layoutOff):
//     recSize, nFields, then nFields field blocks of 6 int32 each (base =
//     layoutOff + 8 + k*24): ftype (RtFtInt/Fixed/Bool/Char/Str/Enum,
//     ser.cla's own numbering), offset (byte offset within the bound
//     record), strCap (RtFtStr only), enumCount (RtFtEnum only),
//     enumLabelsOff, enumValuesOff (both -1 unless RtFtEnum).
//   EnumArrays (contiguous int32s, referenced by a Layout field's own
//     enumLabelsOff/enumValuesOff): enumLabelsOff + n*4 is a Str255
//     blob-absolute offset (a member's label); enumValuesOff + n*4 is the
//     member's raw ordinal `int`.
//
// Every accessor below returns EITHER the raw `int` value (a count, a
// dimension, a bool-as-0/1 field surfaced as `bool`) or, per Task 7 report
// log entry #6 (binding: Str255 data is ALWAYS `ptr`, never `str`, since
// Clarus has no str<->ptr conversion and no address-of for a local `str`),
// a `ptr` for every string-shaped field. String-shaped fields come in two
// forms: a low-level `uidXNameOff(...)` accessor returning the raw blob-
// absolute int offset (or -1) for a caller that needs to test for absence
// itself, and a high-level `uidXName(...)` accessor that bakes in the
// `-1 -> ptr(0)` check (via the shared `uidStrPtr` helper) for the common
// case. Index parameters (winIdx/widgetIdx/menuIdx/itemIdx/k) are NEVER
// bounds-checked here -- unlike rtListAt/rtTextIndex's STRICT-panic
// contract (list.cla/text.cla), which guard genuinely untrusted container
// indices, every index this layer accepts is compiler-generated (a blob
// built by uibBuild from the program's own IR, walked by ported Clarus
// code that only ever iterates 0..uidWinNWidgets(winIdx)-1 and similar) --
// there is no untrusted caller of this layer to guard against, and a
// wrong index here is a Task 7+ bug to fix at the call site, not a runtime
// condition to detect.
//
// `UiProgDesc(): ptr` (the "reverse waist" blob-base extern, Task 6) is
// declared here since this is the first file that needs it -- cprint.cla
// resolves it specially (straight to `&clar_ui_blob[0]`, no rt_ext_ shim,
// no trap/inline clause needed) rather than through the ordinary extern
// dispatch path, so a bare declaration (no `= trap`/`= inline` clause,
// same shape as the language reference's own `external func GetTicks():
// int` example) is correct and complete.
//
// PEEKL DECISION (verified against docs/clarus-language-reference.md's own
// Ch13 text, not assumed): "Multi-byte reads and writes use the machine's
// native byte order -- big-endian on the 68k target. peekw/peekl/pokew/
// pokel are for values already resident in memory in native order (a
// Toolbox struct field, a register image); they are never the right tool
// for a byte layout that leaves the process (a network packet, a saved
// file) -- build that layout with explicit peekb/pokeb calls." The blob is
// EXACTLY that second case: a byte layout with a portable, target-
// independent spec (uiblob.cla's own header comment says cg68k.cla, Task
// 12, will pour these SAME bytes into the native constant pool too -- the
// format's own contract is "big-endian," full stop, not "whatever this
// backend's native order happens to be"), built by uibI32 one byte at a
// time regardless of host/target. peekl's actual behavior tracks the
// EXECUTING machine's order, which only coincides with big-endian on the
// real m68k target this Mac-only module ends up running on -- relying on
// that coincidence would silently break the moment this format is read
// from anywhere else (a differential-testing host build, a future tool).
// So: peekl is NOT used here. `uidI32` below is a hand-rolled big-endian
// reader (4 `peekb` calls + shifts/or), the exact mirror of uibI32's own
// writer (clarusc/uiblob.cla: `b.add(int(char(v >> 24))); ...`) run in
// reverse. The `(b0 << 24) | (b1 << 16) | (b2 << 8) | b3` shift/or idiom
// is already precedented in this codebase for exactly this purpose --
// runtime/clarus/ser.cla's rtSerGetI32 (its own big-endian int32 reader
// for the file-save/load byte format) uses the identical expression.
//
// ================================================================
// RtUiWinst / RtUiCanvasBuf (overlay records over raw allocated memory)
// ================================================================
//
// RtUiWinst is NOT a byte-for-byte port of the C `rt_ui_winst` struct
// (runtime/mac/rt_ui.c:281-327) -- that C struct's bytes are never read by
// anything outside rt_ui.c itself (unlike RtText/RtList, which MUST mirror
// rt_core.inc exactly because both a C reference and Clarus read the same
// bytes), and rt_ui.c is dropped entirely from the build under
// CLARUS_UIPORT=1. This is a fresh design, free to choose its own field
// order, with two binding corrections from Task 7 report log entry #5:
// `desc: const rt_ui_window_desc *` (a raw descriptor pointer) is GONE --
// replaced by `winIdx: int` (the blob's own Window-section 0-based index);
// every `inst->desc->X` read in the C reference becomes a
// `uidWinX(inst.winIdx)`/`uidWidgetX(inst.winIdx, i)` blob-accessor call in
// a LATER step (ui.cla/uiwidgets.cla), never a field read here. `focusIdx`
// is kept (parity with the C struct's slot -- Task 7's own scope note:
// "the STRUCT LAYOUT is shared... uidesc.cla's winst constants must
// reserve their slots even if slice-A code never touches them") but is
// ALWAYS -1 this slice (no FIELD/TEXTVIEW kind can ever be created -- the
// fail-closed gate, a later step). `traceId` is unconditional here (no
// `#ifdef RT_MAC_TEST` -- Clarus has no preprocessor, and every exercised
// path in this port is test-harness-driven anyway).
//
// EXCLUDED (Tasks 9-10's own fields, not this slice's problem):
// popupsH/popups, popupSelH/popupSel, listsH/lists -- POPUP/TABLE-
// specific. teH/tes, hbarsH/hbars (FIELD/TEXTVIEW) are Task 8's own
// addition -- see RtUiWinst below.
//
// Field width: every field below is `int` or `ptr`. Both are EXACTLY 4
// bytes wide as an overlay-record field, unconditionally -- this is a
// LANGUAGE-DEFINED width (Ch13: "an overlay field's offset and width must
// be exactly one of those [int/bool/char/fixed/ptr peek/poke widths]",
// and peekl always reads/writes 4 bytes), not something that varies by
// struct-layout inference the way a raw C `Handle` field's width did for
// rtTextBoxSize/rtListBoxSize (text.cla/list.cla's own doc comments) --
// this module is Mac-ONLY (never compiled for the differential-testing
// host, ui.cla's own header comment), so the only target that ever matters
// is real m68k, where cg68k.cla's own LAYOUT AUTHORITY comment
// (clarusc/cg68k.cla:30-57) already fixes int/ptr at exactly 4 bytes each,
// and Retro68's `#pragma options align=mac68k` (2-byte packing, no padding
// beyond natural alignment -- Task 7 report log entry #4's own derivation)
// packs consecutive 4-byte C fields with zero gaps too, so both backends
// agree: consecutive int/ptr fields occupy exactly 4 bytes apiece with no
// padding between them. `rtUiWinstBoxSize` below is therefore an EXACT
// count (17 fields x 4 = 68), not a struct-layout guess -- unlike
// rtTextBoxSize/rtListBoxSize's real host-vs-target Handle-width
// uncertainty, a small fixed slack is added purely as free insurance
// against a future miscount when a field is added, not because of any
// actual sizing ambiguity here (see the constant's own comment).
//
// RECT / BITMAP HANDLING (the load-bearing decision every later step
// depends on -- Task 7 report log entry #7's own WindowRecord/GrafPort
// offset derivations use the SAME method). A real Toolbox `Rect` is 4
// packed 16-bit SHORTs (8 bytes total: top@0, left@2, bottom@4, right@6 --
// verified directly against Retro68's own header, MacTypes.h:511-516,
// `struct Rect { short top; short left; short bottom; short right; };` --
// NOT left/top/right/bottom, Inside Mac's own field order is top-left-
// bottom-right) and a `BitMap` is 14 bytes (baseAddr: Ptr@0 (4), rowBytes:
// short@4 (2), bounds: Rect@6 (8) -- verified against QuickDraw.h:259-263).
// An `overlay record` CANNOT model either directly: Ch13 restricts overlay
// fields to int/bool/char/fixed/ptr, each a FIXED width the language
// itself defines (4/1/1/4/4 bytes) -- there is no 2-byte overlay field
// type, so a naive `overlay record RtUiRect { top: int, left: int, ... }`
// would read/write 4-byte-strided slots over an 8-byte structure and
// corrupt whatever memory follows it. RESOLUTION: don't use an overlay
// record for either. Toolbox Rect/BitMap memory (RtUiWinst.rects array
// entries, or a scratch BitMap a later step builds for SetPortBits/
// CopyBits) is read/written directly with the BUILT-IN `peekw`/`pokew`/
// `peekb`/`pokeb`/`pokel` -- no custom helper function needed, because
// peekw/pokew are EXACTLY the tool the language spec itself recommends for
// this case ("for values already resident in memory in native order (a
// Toolbox struct field...)" -- ch13, quoted above), and native order IS
// big-endian on this module's only-ever target, m68k, which is ALSO a real
// Toolbox struct's own actual byte order -- no coincidence to worry about
// here, unlike the blob's peekl question above, because a Toolbox struct's
// bytes never leave this one process/target. The exact recipe a later step
// needs, spelled out once here so it never has to be re-derived:
//   - A `rects` array entry (RtUiWinst.rects, window-local Rect,
//     `rtUiRectSize` = 8 bytes/entry): `peekw(r+0)`/`pokew(r+0, ...)` for
//     top, `+2` left, `+4` bottom, `+6` right, where `r = rects + i*8`.
//   - A scratch Toolbox BitMap (from an RtUiCanvasBuf's scattered fields,
//     below) at some 14-byte `ptr bm`: `pokel(bm+0, int(cb.bitsBaseAddr))`
//     (baseAddr, a real Ptr value -- `pokel` is correct here too, it's a
//     native-order in-memory pointer slot, and m68k `ptr` is exactly
//     32 bits so no truncation), `pokew(bm+4, cb.bitsRowBytes)` (rowBytes),
//     `pokew(bm+6, cb.bitsBoundsTop)`, `pokew(bm+8, cb.bitsBoundsLeft)`,
//     `pokew(bm+10, cb.bitsBoundsBottom)`, `pokew(bm+12, cb.bitsBoundsRight)`
//     (bounds, same top/left/bottom/right order as a plain Rect since
//     BitMap.bounds IS a Rect). The 14-byte scratch itself is any `ptr`
//     the caller owns (a `UiNewPtr(14)` the caller allocates once, e.g.
//     per RtUiWinst, and reuses across calls -- not this file's job to
//     allocate, since no Step-2 function ever needs to actually issue a
//     SetPortBits/CopyBits call).
//
// RtUiCanvasBuf mirrors the C `rt_ui_canvas_buf` (rt_ui.c:279) with
// SCATTERED fields (the other of the two options Task 7's own brief
// offers) rather than an embedded 14-byte BitMap-shaped region -- chosen
// BECAUSE overlay records can't express BitMap's 2-byte rowBytes field
// (same restriction as Rect, immediately above), so there is no way to
// embed a real BitMap shape in an overlay record at all; scattering into
// plain `int`/`ptr` overlay fields (each a full, unambiguous 4-byte slot)
// and building a temporary scratch BitMap on demand (recipe above) is the
// only option Ch13's field-type restriction leaves open. `port: ptr`
// doubles as the C reference's own "is this canvas buffered and
// allocated" flag (`ptr(0)` for every non-canvas widget and every
// unbuffered canvas) -- same convention, ported as-is. `rtUiCanvasBufSize`
// (44 bytes = 11 fields x 4, see above for why int/ptr fields are exactly 4
// bytes each) is a STRIDE for indexing `canvases[i]`, not an allocation
// safety margin like rtUiWinstBoxSize -- it must stay EXACT (no slack),
// or every `canvases + i*rtUiCanvasBufSize` computation later steps make
// misaligns against the next element.

// ---- The reverse-waist blob-base extern (Task 6) ----
external func UiProgDesc(): ptr

// ---- Big-endian int32 reader (see "PEEKL DECISION" above) ----
func uidI32(p: ptr): int {
    var b0: int
    var b1: int
    var b2: int
    var b3: int

    b0 = peekb(p)
    b1 = peekb(p + 1)
    b2 = peekb(p + 2)
    b3 = peekb(p + 3)
    return (b0 << 24) | (b1 << 16) | (b2 << 8) | b3
}

// uidStrPtr turns a blob-absolute Str255 byte offset (or -1, "absent") into
// a `ptr` a caller can pass straight to a Pascal-string Toolbox trap or a
// text.cla/etc conversion helper -- the shared "-1 -> ptr(0)" check every
// high-level Name/Title/Caption/Label accessor below bakes in.
func uidStrPtr(off: int): ptr {
    if off == -1 {
        return ptr(0)
    }
    return UiProgDesc() + off
}

// ================================================================
// Header (11 int32 @ 0, 4, .., 40)
// ================================================================

func uidNWins(): int {
    return uidI32(UiProgDesc() + 8)
}

func uidWinsOff(): int {
    return uidI32(UiProgDesc() + 12)
}

func uidNMenus(): int {
    return uidI32(UiProgDesc() + 16)
}

func uidMenusOff(): int {
    return uidI32(UiProgDesc() + 20)
}

func uidNMenuHandlers(): int {
    return uidI32(UiProgDesc() + 24)
}

func uidMhOff(): int {
    return uidI32(UiProgDesc() + 28)
}

func uidNEvery(): int {
    return uidI32(UiProgDesc() + 32)
}

func uidEveryOff(): int {
    return uidI32(UiProgDesc() + 36)
}

func uidAppOff(): int {
    return uidI32(UiProgDesc() + 40)
}

// ================================================================
// Window (13 int32, 52 bytes/entry, base = winsOff + winIdx*52)
// ================================================================

func uidWinBase(winIdx: int): ptr {
    return UiProgDesc() + uidWinsOff() + winIdx * 52
}

func uidWinNameOff(winIdx: int): int {
    return uidI32(uidWinBase(winIdx) + 0)
}

func uidWinName(winIdx: int): ptr {
    return uidStrPtr(uidWinNameOff(winIdx))
}

func uidWinTitleOff(winIdx: int): int {
    return uidI32(uidWinBase(winIdx) + 4)
}

func uidWinTitle(winIdx: int): ptr {
    return uidStrPtr(uidWinTitleOff(winIdx))
}

func uidWinW(winIdx: int): int {
    return uidI32(uidWinBase(winIdx) + 8)
}

func uidWinH(winIdx: int): int {
    return uidI32(uidWinBase(winIdx) + 12)
}

func uidWinResizable(winIdx: int): bool {
    return uidI32(uidWinBase(winIdx) + 16) != 0
}

func uidWinMinW(winIdx: int): int {
    return uidI32(uidWinBase(winIdx) + 20)
}

func uidWinMinH(winIdx: int): int {
    return uidI32(uidWinBase(winIdx) + 24)
}

func uidWinNWidgets(winIdx: int): int {
    return uidI32(uidWinBase(winIdx) + 28)
}

func uidWinWidgetsOff(winIdx: int): int {
    return uidI32(uidWinBase(winIdx) + 32)
}

func uidWinStateSize(winIdx: int): int {
    return uidI32(uidWinBase(winIdx) + 36)
}

func uidWinHandlerMask(winIdx: int): int {
    return uidI32(uidWinBase(winIdx) + 40)
}

func uidWinFormOff(winIdx: int): int {
    return uidI32(uidWinBase(winIdx) + 44)
}

// uidWinMenuMask (language-runtime-cleanup, spec 5.3): the set of menus
// this window TYPE claims via `menus:`, as a bitmask over whole-program
// menu declaration indices. rtUiSyncMenuBar ORs the front window's mask
// onto the app-scope set.
func uidWinMenuMask(winIdx: int): int {
    return uidI32(uidWinBase(winIdx) + 48)
}

// ================================================================
// Widget (13 int32, 52 bytes/entry, base = widgetsOff + widgetIdx*52)
// ================================================================

func uidWidgetBase(winIdx: int, widgetIdx: int): ptr {
    return UiProgDesc() + uidWinWidgetsOff(winIdx) + widgetIdx * 52
}

func uidWidgetKind(winIdx: int, widgetIdx: int): int {
    return uidI32(uidWidgetBase(winIdx, widgetIdx) + 0)
}

func uidWidgetNameOff(winIdx: int, widgetIdx: int): int {
    return uidI32(uidWidgetBase(winIdx, widgetIdx) + 4)
}

func uidWidgetName(winIdx: int, widgetIdx: int): ptr {
    return uidStrPtr(uidWidgetNameOff(winIdx, widgetIdx))
}

func uidWidgetCaptionOff(winIdx: int, widgetIdx: int): int {
    return uidI32(uidWidgetBase(winIdx, widgetIdx) + 8)
}

func uidWidgetCaption(winIdx: int, widgetIdx: int): ptr {
    return uidStrPtr(uidWidgetCaptionOff(winIdx, widgetIdx))
}

func uidWidgetAtKind(winIdx: int, widgetIdx: int): int {
    return uidI32(uidWidgetBase(winIdx, widgetIdx) + 12)
}

func uidWidgetX(winIdx: int, widgetIdx: int): int {
    return uidI32(uidWidgetBase(winIdx, widgetIdx) + 16)
}

func uidWidgetYIsBottom(winIdx: int, widgetIdx: int): bool {
    return uidI32(uidWidgetBase(winIdx, widgetIdx) + 20) != 0
}

func uidWidgetY(winIdx: int, widgetIdx: int): int {
    return uidI32(uidWidgetBase(winIdx, widgetIdx) + 24)
}

func uidWidgetWidthIsFill(winIdx: int, widgetIdx: int): bool {
    return uidI32(uidWidgetBase(winIdx, widgetIdx) + 28) != 0
}

func uidWidgetWidth(winIdx: int, widgetIdx: int): int {
    return uidI32(uidWidgetBase(winIdx, widgetIdx) + 32)
}

func uidWidgetFillBoth(winIdx: int, widgetIdx: int): bool {
    return uidI32(uidWidgetBase(winIdx, widgetIdx) + 36) != 0
}

func uidWidgetFlags(winIdx: int, widgetIdx: int): int {
    return uidI32(uidWidgetBase(winIdx, widgetIdx) + 40)
}

func uidWidgetEventMask(winIdx: int, widgetIdx: int): int {
    return uidI32(uidWidgetBase(winIdx, widgetIdx) + 44)
}

func uidWidgetTableOff(winIdx: int, widgetIdx: int): int {
    return uidI32(uidWidgetBase(winIdx, widgetIdx) + 48)
}

// ================================================================
// Menu (5 int32, 20 bytes/entry, base = menusOff + menuIdx*20)
// ================================================================

func uidMenuBase(menuIdx: int): ptr {
    return UiProgDesc() + uidMenusOff() + menuIdx * 20
}

func uidMenuNameOff(menuIdx: int): int {
    return uidI32(uidMenuBase(menuIdx) + 0)
}

func uidMenuName(menuIdx: int): ptr {
    return uidStrPtr(uidMenuNameOff(menuIdx))
}

func uidMenuTitleOff(menuIdx: int): int {
    return uidI32(uidMenuBase(menuIdx) + 4)
}

func uidMenuTitle(menuIdx: int): ptr {
    return uidStrPtr(uidMenuTitleOff(menuIdx))
}

func uidMenuNItems(menuIdx: int): int {
    return uidI32(uidMenuBase(menuIdx) + 8)
}

func uidMenuItemsOff(menuIdx: int): int {
    return uidI32(uidMenuBase(menuIdx) + 12)
}

func uidMenuIsStandardEdit(menuIdx: int): bool {
    return uidI32(uidMenuBase(menuIdx) + 16) != 0
}

// ================================================================
// Item (4 int32, 16 bytes/entry, base = a menu's own itemsOff +
// itemIdx*16) -- indexed (menuIdx, itemIdx) rather than a bare items-
// section-global index, matching Widget's own (winIdx, widgetIdx) shape:
// callers always have menuIdx on hand (iterating a menu's own items) and
// this spares them re-deriving itemsOff themselves.
// ================================================================

func uidItemBase(menuIdx: int, itemIdx: int): ptr {
    return UiProgDesc() + uidMenuItemsOff(menuIdx) + itemIdx * 16
}

func uidItemNameOff(menuIdx: int, itemIdx: int): int {
    return uidI32(uidItemBase(menuIdx, itemIdx) + 0)
}

func uidItemName(menuIdx: int, itemIdx: int): ptr {
    return uidStrPtr(uidItemNameOff(menuIdx, itemIdx))
}

func uidItemLabelOff(menuIdx: int, itemIdx: int): int {
    return uidI32(uidItemBase(menuIdx, itemIdx) + 4)
}

func uidItemLabel(menuIdx: int, itemIdx: int): ptr {
    return uidStrPtr(uidItemLabelOff(menuIdx, itemIdx))
}

func uidItemKey(menuIdx: int, itemIdx: int): int {
    return uidI32(uidItemBase(menuIdx, itemIdx) + 8)
}

func uidItemSeparator(menuIdx: int, itemIdx: int): bool {
    return uidI32(uidItemBase(menuIdx, itemIdx) + 12) != 0
}

// ================================================================
// MenuHandler (6 int32, 24 bytes/entry, base = mhOff + k*24) -- k is the
// handler's own 0-based position in the array (uiblob.cla's own
// `handlerIdx` field mirrors k, by construction: uibBuild increments it
// once per entry in emission order).
// ================================================================

func uidMenuHandlerBase(k: int): ptr {
    return UiProgDesc() + uidMhOff() + k * 24
}

func uidMenuHandlerMenuIdx(k: int): int {
    return uidI32(uidMenuHandlerBase(k) + 0)
}

func uidMenuHandlerItemIdx(k: int): int {
    return uidI32(uidMenuHandlerBase(k) + 4)
}

func uidMenuHandlerHandlerIdx(k: int): int {
    return uidI32(uidMenuHandlerBase(k) + 8)
}

func uidMenuHandlerScopeWinIdx(k: int): int {
    return uidI32(uidMenuHandlerBase(k) + 12)
}

func uidMenuHandlerMenuNameOff(k: int): int {
    return uidI32(uidMenuHandlerBase(k) + 16)
}

func uidMenuHandlerMenuName(k: int): ptr {
    return uidStrPtr(uidMenuHandlerMenuNameOff(k))
}

func uidMenuHandlerItemNameOff(k: int): int {
    return uidI32(uidMenuHandlerBase(k) + 20)
}

func uidMenuHandlerItemName(k: int): ptr {
    return uidStrPtr(uidMenuHandlerItemNameOff(k))
}

// ================================================================
// Every (1 int32, 4 bytes/entry, base = everyOff + k*4)
// ================================================================

func uidEveryTicks(k: int): int {
    return uidI32(UiProgDesc() + uidEveryOff() + k * 4)
}

// ================================================================
// App (4 int32, 16 bytes, single instance at appOff -- absent, -1, when
// the program declares no `app` section)
// ================================================================

func uidHasApp(): bool {
    return uidAppOff() != -1
}

func uidAppNameOff(): int {
    if not uidHasApp() {
        return -1
    }
    return uidI32(UiProgDesc() + uidAppOff() + 0)
}

func uidAppName(): ptr {
    return uidStrPtr(uidAppNameOff())
}

func uidAppVersionOff(): int {
    if not uidHasApp() {
        return -1
    }
    return uidI32(UiProgDesc() + uidAppOff() + 4)
}

func uidAppVersion(): ptr {
    return uidStrPtr(uidAppVersionOff())
}

func uidAppAuthorOff(): int {
    if not uidHasApp() {
        return -1
    }
    return uidI32(UiProgDesc() + uidAppOff() + 8)
}

func uidAppAuthor(): ptr {
    return uidStrPtr(uidAppAuthorOff())
}

func uidAppAboutOff(): int {
    if not uidHasApp() {
        return -1
    }
    return uidI32(UiProgDesc() + uidAppOff() + 12)
}

func uidAppAbout(): ptr {
    return uidStrPtr(uidAppAboutOff())
}

// ================================================================
// Form (3 int32, 12 bytes, at a window's own formOff -- uidWinFormOff
// above) -- Task 9. `off` here (and everywhere below through EnumArrays)
// is always a blob-ABSOLUTE byte offset already resolved by the caller
// through a parent field (uidWinFormOff, uidWidgetTableOff, a Table's own
// layoutOff/colsOff, a Layout field's own enumLabelsOff/enumValuesOff) --
// there is no separate "section base" to add, by construction
// (clarusc/uiblob.cla's own uibEmitForm/uibEmitTable/uibEmitLayout each
// return the absolute offset they just wrote).
// ================================================================

func uidFormLayoutOff(off: int): int {
    return uidI32(UiProgDesc() + off + 0)
}

func uidFormNBinds(off: int): int {
    return uidI32(UiProgDesc() + off + 4)
}

func uidFormBindsOff(off: int): int {
    return uidI32(UiProgDesc() + off + 8)
}

// ================================================================
// Bind (2 int32, 8 bytes/entry, base = a form's own bindsOff + k*8)
// ================================================================

func uidBindBase(bindsOff: int, k: int): ptr {
    return UiProgDesc() + bindsOff + k * 8
}

func uidBindWidgetIndex(bindsOff: int, k: int): int {
    return uidI32(uidBindBase(bindsOff, k) + 0)
}

func uidBindFieldIndex(bindsOff: int, k: int): int {
    return uidI32(uidBindBase(bindsOff, k) + 4)
}

// uidFormFindFieldIndex scans form `off`'s own binds for the one whose
// widgetIndex == widgetIdx, returning its fieldIndex (an index into the
// SAME form's own uidFormLayoutOff layout) -- -1 if `off` is -1 (no form)
// or no bind matches (rt_ui.h's defensive "unbound widget" contract, same
// shape as rt_ui_make_widgets' own popup-creation loop, rt_ui.c:1273-1299).
func uidFormFindFieldIndex(off: int, widgetIdx: int): int {
    var bindsOff: int
    var nBinds: int
    var b: int

    if off == -1 {
        return -1
    }
    bindsOff = uidFormBindsOff(off)
    nBinds = uidFormNBinds(off)
    b = 0
    while b < nBinds {
        if uidBindWidgetIndex(bindsOff, b) == widgetIdx {
            return uidBindFieldIndex(bindsOff, b)
        }
        b = b + 1
    }
    return -1
}

// ================================================================
// Table (4 int32, 16 bytes, at a widget's own tableOff -- uidWidgetTableOff
// above)
// ================================================================

func uidTableRowsIdx(off: int): int {
    return uidI32(UiProgDesc() + off + 0)
}

func uidTableLayoutOff(off: int): int {
    return uidI32(UiProgDesc() + off + 4)
}

func uidTableNCols(off: int): int {
    return uidI32(UiProgDesc() + off + 8)
}

func uidTableColsOff(off: int): int {
    return uidI32(UiProgDesc() + off + 12)
}

// ================================================================
// Col (4 int32, 16 bytes/entry, base = a table's own colsOff + k*16)
// ================================================================

func uidColBase(colsOff: int, k: int): ptr {
    return UiProgDesc() + colsOff + k * 16
}

func uidColHeaderOff(colsOff: int, k: int): int {
    return uidI32(uidColBase(colsOff, k) + 0)
}

func uidColHeader(colsOff: int, k: int): ptr {
    return uidStrPtr(uidColHeaderOff(colsOff, k))
}

func uidColWidthPx(colsOff: int, k: int): int {
    return uidI32(uidColBase(colsOff, k) + 4)
}

func uidColWidthFill(colsOff: int, k: int): bool {
    return uidI32(uidColBase(colsOff, k) + 8) != 0
}

func uidColFieldIndex(colsOff: int, k: int): int {
    return uidI32(uidColBase(colsOff, k) + 12)
}

// ================================================================
// Layout (2 + nFields*6 int32, at a form's or table's own layoutOff):
// recSize@+0, nFields@+4, then nFields field blocks of 6 int32 (24
// bytes) each starting at layoutOff+8.
// ================================================================

func uidLayoutRecSize(off: int): int {
    return uidI32(UiProgDesc() + off + 0)
}

func uidLayoutNFields(off: int): int {
    return uidI32(UiProgDesc() + off + 4)
}

func uidFieldBase(layoutOff: int, k: int): ptr {
    return UiProgDesc() + layoutOff + 8 + k * 24
}

func uidFieldFtype(layoutOff: int, k: int): int {
    return uidI32(uidFieldBase(layoutOff, k) + 0)
}

func uidFieldOffset(layoutOff: int, k: int): int {
    return uidI32(uidFieldBase(layoutOff, k) + 4)
}

func uidFieldStrCap(layoutOff: int, k: int): int {
    return uidI32(uidFieldBase(layoutOff, k) + 8)
}

func uidFieldEnumCount(layoutOff: int, k: int): int {
    return uidI32(uidFieldBase(layoutOff, k) + 12)
}

func uidFieldEnumLabelsOff(layoutOff: int, k: int): int {
    return uidI32(uidFieldBase(layoutOff, k) + 16)
}

func uidFieldEnumValuesOff(layoutOff: int, k: int): int {
    return uidI32(uidFieldBase(layoutOff, k) + 20)
}

// ================================================================
// EnumArrays (contiguous int32s -- a Layout field's own enumLabelsOff/
// enumValuesOff, EACH already blob-absolute; `n` is 0..enumCount-1).
// ================================================================

func uidEnumLabelOff(enumLabelsOff: int, n: int): int {
    return uidI32(UiProgDesc() + enumLabelsOff + n * 4)
}

func uidEnumLabel(enumLabelsOff: int, n: int): ptr {
    return uidStrPtr(uidEnumLabelOff(enumLabelsOff, n))
}

func uidEnumValue(enumValuesOff: int, n: int): int {
    return uidI32(UiProgDesc() + enumValuesOff + n * 4)
}

// ================================================================
// RtUiWinst / RtUiCanvasBuf overlay records -- see the file header
// comment's own section for the full design rationale.
// ================================================================

overlay record RtUiWinst {
    selfH: ptr           // this struct's own locked box (rt_ui.c:282)
    wp: ptr               // the WindowPtr (rt_ui.c:283)
    winIdx: int           // blob Window-section index -- REPLACES the C
                           // reference's `desc` pointer (report log #5)
    stateH: ptr            // per-instance user vars box, or ptr(0) if
    state: ptr             // stateSize==0 (rt_ui.c:285)
    ctrlsH: ptr             // nWidgets ControlHandles; ptr(0) entries for
    ctrls: ptr              // non-Control kinds (rt_ui.c:286)
    rectsH: ptr              // nWidgets Rects (8 bytes/entry, see the file
    rects: ptr               // header's Rect recipe), window-local coords
    labelsH: ptr              // nWidgets 256-byte Str255 slots; only LABEL
    labels: ptr                // kinds used (rt_ui.c:288)
    canvasH: ptr                 // nWidgets RtUiCanvasBuf records; only
    canvases: ptr                 // buffered CANVAS kinds used (rt_ui.c:289)
    // Task 8: TextEdit field/textview widgets (rt_ui.c:304-305). `tes[i]`
    // holds one TEHandle per widget (ptr(0) for every non-FIELD/TEXTVIEW
    // kind); a textview's own vertical scrollbar (RTUI_SCROLL_V) is a
    // normal Control Manager control living in `ctrls[i]`, same array a
    // button/check use -- `hbars[i]` (below) is ONLY the horizontal
    // scrollbar (RTUI_SCROLL_H). Placed here, between canvasH/canvases and
    // enabledH/logicalEnabled, mirroring the C reference's own field order
    // (not required for a from-scratch overlay design -- see the file
    // header comment -- but kept for cross-reference clarity against the
    // oracle).
    teH: ptr
    tes: ptr
    hbarsH: ptr
    hbars: ptr                    // nWidgets; textview H scrollbar or ptr(0)
    enabledH: ptr                  // nWidgets bytes: PROGRAM-set ENABLED
    logicalEnabled: ptr              // state, independent of window dim
    focusIdx: int                      // the one FIELD/TEXTVIEW with the
                                        // caret, -1 = none (Task 8: now
                                        // live -- see uitext.cla)
    traceId: int                       // unconditional here (no #ifdef --
                                        // Clarus has no preprocessor)
    // Task 9: List Manager tables + manual popups (uitable.cla). `lists[i]`
    // holds one ListHandle per TABLE-kind widget (ptr(0) otherwise);
    // `popups[i]`/`popupSel[i]` hold one MenuHandle / current 0-based item
    // index per POPUP-kind widget (ptr(0)/0 otherwise -- NewHandleClear's
    // own zero-fill already gives every widget this default for free, same
    // as ctrls/tes/hbars need no explicit per-widget zero loop). Placed
    // last (not interleaved with the Task 7/8 fields above, unlike those
    // two's own cross-reference-to-the-C-struct placement) since there is
    // no C struct field order left to mirror here -- rt_ui_winst's own
    // lists/popups/popupSel fields sit BEFORE its enabled/focusIdx/traceId
    // ones (rt_ui.c:281-327), but this port's field order was already a
    // fresh design by Task 7 (file header comment), so appending is the
    // smaller diff.
    listsH: ptr
    lists: ptr
    popupsH: ptr
    popups: ptr
    popupSelH: ptr
    popupSel: ptr
}

// rtUiWinstBoxSize is UiNewPtr's argument for allocating an RtUiWinst box
// (via rtUiAllocLocked, a later step). EXACT, not a guess: 27 fields (Task
// 9 added listsH/lists/popupsH/popups/popupSelH/popupSel), each exactly 4
// bytes as an overlay-record field (see the file header comment, "Field
// width" paragraph) = 108 bytes, +12 bytes fixed slack (rounding to 120)
// purely as free insurance against a future field-count edit, not because
// of any real sizing ambiguity the way rtTextBoxSize/rtListBoxSize
// (text.cla/list.cla) have for their own C-struct `Handle`-width question.
const rtUiWinstBoxSize: int = 120

// RtUiCanvasBuf: SCATTERED fields (not an embedded BitMap-shaped region --
// see the file header comment's "RECT / BITMAP HANDLING" section for why
// an overlay record can't express one). `port` doubles as the "buffered
// and allocated" flag, `ptr(0)` for every non-canvas widget and every
// unbuffered canvas -- same convention as the C reference (rt_ui.c:279).
overlay record RtUiCanvasBuf {
    port: ptr             // the offscreen GrafPort record, or ptr(0)
    bitsBaseAddr: ptr       // scratch BitMap.baseAddr (see recipe above)
    bitsRowBytes: int        // scratch BitMap.rowBytes
    bitsBoundsTop: int        // scratch BitMap.bounds (a Rect: top/left/
    bitsBoundsLeft: int        // bottom/right, same field order as a real
    bitsBoundsBottom: int       // Toolbox Rect)
    bitsBoundsRight: int
    pixels: ptr                  // the offscreen pixel data block
    patLevel: int                  // gray-pattern animation level
    dirty: int                     // language-runtime-cleanup: 1 = drawn since last blit
    blits: int                     // language-runtime-cleanup: blit counter (UiTestCanvasBlits)
}

// rtUiCanvasBufSize is the per-element STRIDE for indexing an
// RtUiWinst.canvases array (`canvases + i*rtUiCanvasBufSize`) -- unlike
// rtUiWinstBoxSize above, this MUST stay exact (11 fields x 4 = 44, no
// slack): padding it would misalign every element after the first.
const rtUiCanvasBufSize: int = 44

// rtUiRectSize is the per-element stride for an RtUiWinst.rects array (a
// real Toolbox Rect, 8 bytes: top/left/bottom/right, each a 16-bit SHORT
// -- see the file header's Rect recipe). Exact, no slack, same reasoning
// as rtUiCanvasBufSize.
const rtUiRectSize: int = 8

// rtUiLabelSlotSize is the per-element stride for an RtUiWinst.labels
// array (one 256-byte Str255 slot per widget -- a 1-byte length prefix
// plus up to 255 content bytes, rt_ui.c:288's own `unsigned char
// (*labels)[256]`). Exact, no slack, same reasoning as rtUiCanvasBufSize.
const rtUiLabelSlotSize: int = 256

// ================================================================
// rtUiAllocLocked -- mirrors rt_ui_alloc_locked (rt_ui.c:334-342) exactly:
// a zero-filled Handle that never moves for the rest of its lifetime
// (locked immediately, stays locked until whatever later step disposes
// it), panicking ("out of memory") on failure exactly like the C
// reference and every other *NewHandle*/*NewPtr* birth call in this
// codebase (TextNewPtr/TextNewHandle et al., text.cla's own header
// comment).
//
// CONTRACT (binding for every caller, Step 4's rtUiOpen the first --
// rtUiOpen makes ~9 of these calls in a row): returns the Handle `h`
// ALONE, never the locked master pointer. Rationale: a caller almost
// always needs the HANDLE far longer than the momentary pointer -- it's
// what gets stored into an RtUiWinst field pair (`inst.ctrlsH = h`) and
// what a later rtUiClose eventually disposes -- while the pointer is only
// ever needed transiently, right after allocating (to poke initial
// contents in) or right before a Toolbox call. Re-deriving it via
// `UiHandleDeref(h)` at each such use site is exactly the relocation
// discipline every other Handle-backed module here already imposes on
// itself (RtText/RtList's own header comments: "never caches a
// dereferenced pointer across a call that can allocate/move memory") --
// returning the pointer instead of the Handle would tempt a caller to
// cache it across a LATER rtUiAllocLocked call, which (being itself a
// NewHandleClear) can move other relocatable blocks via heap compaction,
// a real bug. The one-line idiom a caller needing the pointer immediately
// uses: `h = rtUiAllocLocked(sz); mp = UiHandleDeref(h)`.
func rtUiAllocLocked(sz: int): ptr {
    var h: ptr

    h = UiNewHandleClear(sz)
    if h == ptr(0) {
        rtPanic("out of memory")
    }
    UiHLock(h)
    return h
}
